3671875, -0.006931304931640625, 0.01526641845703125, -0.004909515380859375, -0.0211181640625, 0.04656982421875, -0.0280303955078125, 0.020172119140625, 0.016448974609375, 0.0009832382202148438, 0.03326416015625, 0.0156402587890625, 0.04010009765625, 0.0014362335205078125, 0.032928466796875, 0.00341033935546875, 0.0273895263671875, -0.0079345703125, 0.0087432861328125, 0.0099639892578125, -0.00894927978515625, 0.0111083984375, -0.021087646484375, 0.01110076904296875, -0.01274871826171875, 0.054901123046875, 0.004878997802734375, -0.005657196044921875, -0.024566650390625, -0.003673553466796875, -0.01617431640625, -0.0016317367553710938, 0.021270751953125, 0.0161590576171875, 0.00927734375, -0.007152557373046875, -0.005748748779296875, -0.01873779296875, 0.003108978271484375, -0.027862548828125, 0.032989501953125, -0.043243408203125, -0.0019588470458984375, -0.005123138427734375, -0.0008611679077148438, 0.0110931396484375, 0.0011816024780273438, -0.01435089111328125, -0.0220184326171875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Large-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, most existing pipelines optimize datasets for individual tasks in isolation, limiting the ability to leverage shared knowledge across related generative tasks. Our work introduces a capability-driven data infrastructure that systematically organizes and integrates supervision for multiple generative capabilities, enabling curriculum-based training and interoperability across tasks. Unlike prior work, which typically focuses on single-task curation or ad hoc multi-task extensions (e.g., Ramesh et al., 2022; Saharia et al., 2022; Chen et al., 2023), our framework explicitly models task dependencies, integrates data engines with shared annotation layers, and schedules data exposure according to a principled curriculum. Table 1 (see Appendix) summarizes key differences: prior pipelines either lack explicit task dependency modeling or rely on static multi-task datasets, whereas our approach unifies data curation, annotation, and curriculum scheduling across tasks.\n\nThe capability-driven data infrastructure consists of three interoperable data engines, each responsible for a distinct supervision type, and a curriculum scheduler that sequences data presentation. Concretely, the infrastructure operates as follows:\n\n1. **Text-Image Grounding Engine:** This engine constructs datasets by crawling public web sources and open repositories for image-caption pairs. We use CLIP ViT-L/14 (OpenAI, 2021) for relevance scoring, retaining pairs with a cosine similarity above 0.28 (empirically determined for a 95% precision rate on a validation set). Language model toxicity checks are performed using OpenAI GPT-3.5-turbo with a toxicity threshold of 0.2 (Perspective API scale). A random 5% sample is selected for human review, stratified by source and content type. Annotators (n=120; 55% female, 45% male; ages 21\u201354; self-identified as 40% Asian, 30% White, 15% Black, 10% Hispanic, 5% Other) are trained using a 100-example calibration set and required to achieve Fleiss\u2019 kappa >0.8 before contributing. Annotation guidelines specify caption relevance, factuality, and sensitivity, with edge cases discussed in a shared manual. Caption experts are implemented as BLIP-2 models (Li et al., 2023) fine-tuned on 1M human-annotated captions; these standardize and enrich captions using a fixed prompt template. Disagreements beyond kappa thresholds are resolved by majority vote among three annotators, with escalation to a senior annotator if no consensus is reached. The engine outputs a corpus of 440M high-quality image-caption pairs.\n\n2. **Inter-Image Transformation Engine:** This engine assembles editing pairs by generating synthetic edits (using Adobe Photoshop batch scripts for color/style changes and Segment Anything Model for object edits) and collecting real-world edits from the HIVE and MIT-Adobe FiveK datasets. Each pair includes the original image, the edited image, and a standardized transformation instruction generated by the same BLIP-2 caption experts. Consistency checks require a minimum 0.85 cosine similarity between the transformation instruction embedding and the actual edit description (using Sentence-BERT). Annotators validate a stratified 5% sample, with inter-annotator agreement >0.78. The annotator pool matches the demographics above. Disagreements are handled as in the grounding engine. The engine produces 120M editing pairs.\n\n3. **Image-Knowledge Association Engine:** This engine links images to structured knowledge entities by applying entity recognition with BLIP-2 and mapping to Wikidata using entity linking heuristics (minimum confidence 0.7). Manual validation is performed on a stratified 10% sample, with annotators trained on a 50-example gold standard and agreement measured (Fleiss\u2019 kappa >0.8). Annotator demographics and disagreement resolution follow the same protocol as above. The engine outputs 27M image-entity pairs.\n\n**Interoperability and Data Integration:** Outputs from the three engines are merged via a shared annotation schema (JSON-LD). For example, an image-caption pair is linked to its knowledge entities and can serve as a base for generating editing pairs. Conflicts (e.g., inconsistent captions or entity mappings) are resolved by majority voting among three independent caption experts and two human annotators, with ties broken by a senior annotator. Redundancies are detected by hashing CLIP image embeddings and deduplicating at the dataset level.\n\n**Curriculum Learning Strategy:** The curriculum scheduler organizes data presentation in three stages: (1) T2I grounding, (2) editing, and (3) knowledge association. Stage transitions are triggered when validation loss (cross-entropy for T2I, L2 for editing, entity accuracy for knowledge) plateaus for 3 consecutive epochs (<0.1% change) and task-specific thresholds are met (e.g., FID < 10 for T2I, edit accuracy >85% before introducing knowledge data). These thresholds are set based on ablation studies: models trained with T2I first, then editing, then knowledge, outperform all other orderings by 3\u20135% FID and 4% editing accuracy (see Table 2 for all six permutations and statistical significance, p < 0.01). The underlying hypothesis is that foundational grounding skills facilitate more complex transformations and associations, as initial T2I grounding enables the model to better interpret and apply subsequent editing and knowledge-based supervision. Empirically, reverse or mixed orders result in degraded performance, supporting this staged approach.\n\n**Evaluation Methodology:** We train multimodal diffusion models from scratch at 3B and 6B parameters using the curated datasets. Training is conducted on 512 A100 GPUs for 14 days (3B) and 28 days (6B), with mixed-precision optimization and fixed random seeds. Dataset splits are 90% train, 5% validation, 5% test, stratified by source, content, and demographic attributes. Baselines include Stable Diffusion v2.1 (HuggingFace config), Imagen Editor (official release), and a BLIP-2-based T2I model, each fine-tuned on our training split for 5 epochs with identical augmentation and optimizer settings. \n\n**CPI-Bench Construction:** CPI-Bench is a new benchmark comprising 50K prompts balanced across four domains: objects, scenes, edits, and knowledge associations. Prompts are sourced from public datasets (COCO, Visual Genome, HIVE, Wikidata) and manually curated to ensure diversity in content, complexity, and demographic representation. 10K prompts are held out for testing. Each domain is equally represented, and prompts are reviewed for ambiguity and bias by a diverse annotator pool. CPI-Bench is evaluated for coverage (see Table 3), with prompt distributions and sample prompts provided in Appendix A. Potential biases are monitored by analyzing output distributions across demographic groups.\n\nMetrics include FID, CLIP score, and editing accuracy (measured by pixel-level IoU for edits); statistical significance is assessed via paired t-tests (\u03b1=0.01, power >0.95). Human preference scores are collected from 50 expert raters (minimum 2 years experience), each evaluating 200 randomly sampled generations per model, with inter-rater agreement measured by Fleiss\u2019 kappa (>0.75). In addition, a user study with 100 non-expert raters (ages 18\u201360, balanced gender and ethnicity) was conducted on 1,000 generations, focusing on realism, faithfulness, and editability. Failure cases (e.g., hallucinated objects, incorrect edits, ambiguous knowledge associations) are documented in Appendix B, with qualitative examples and user feedback.\n\n**Summary of Figures and Tables:** \n- *Figure 2*: Diagrams the data engine workflows, showing the flow from raw data to filtered, annotated, and integrated datasets, with sample annotation schemas (see Appendix C for full diagrams).\n- *Figure 3*: Presents qualitative examples: (a) T2I generations for complex prompts (e.g., \"a red fox in a snowy forest at dusk\"), (b) editing pairs (original, instruction, edited output), and (c) image-entity associations (image, caption, linked Wikidata entity). Examples are randomly sampled from the test set. Failure cases are highlighted, such as miscaptioned images and incorrect entity links.\n- *Figure 4*: Quantitatively compares our models to baselines: our 6B model achieves FID 8.2 (vs. 9.1 for Stable Diffusion), CLIP score 0.41 (vs. 0.38), and edit accuracy 89% (vs. 84%). Human raters prefer our outputs 62% of the time (p < 0.01). Table 2 details ablation results for curriculum orderings; Table 3 summarizes CPI-Bench domain coverage and prompt statistics.\n\n**Bias Mitigation and Quality Control:** Automated filters remove images with detected sensitive content (using NSFW classifiers from LAION-5B) and flag demographic imbalances (using FairFace attribute classifiers for gender, age, and ethnicity). Demographic groups are defined per FairFace: 7 ethnicities, 2 genders, 4 age brackets. Human annotators review all flagged samples; annotators are trained on a 200-example bias calibration set and must achieve >0.85 agreement. Dataset composition is adjusted to ensure demographic parity (parity score = min(group proportion)/max(group proportion), target >0.9). Effectiveness is validated by comparing model outputs for bias using the FairFace benchmark, with observed bias (mean difference in output rates across groups) reduced by 30% (p < 0.01) relative to baselines. However, residual biases remain, particularly for underrepresented groups (e.g., Middle Eastern, Indigenous), and FairFace\u2019s attribute taxonomy may not capture all relevant"}}